id-2010s\" (2015-2020 average 39.1 vs 2000s 31.6 vs 1960s 17.2). Good.\n\nMaybe one fact: yearly percentage negative 1960-2020 (printout all years). Claim can say it climbs from X in 1960 to Y in 2020 and averages Z by decade. But the claim should not be a vague descriptor; numbers the code prints. Fine.\n\nFact: 2020 outnumber positive — false, skip. But wait: maybe we should compute \"year-end top 10\" as the top 10 of the Billboard year-end chart, which is not the same as weekly top 10. Data lacks year-end chart positions. Skip with reason: data shows 49.2% negative vs 50.8% positive in 2020, and 2018 had 55.6% negative; not reproduced.\n\nFact 3: joy most common individual emotion, on relative decline. Compute mean or total counts per year; joy is most common overall, but in 2018 anger and 2020 fear were most common per-year by total count. The claim \"Joy, while still the most common individual emotion\" — overall (1960-2020) joy total 29.31 mean vs trust 29.27, very close. Actually joy just barely top. \"Has been on a relative decline\": joy's share of emotion words declines from 1960s? Compute decade mean joy / total. Decade mean counts: joy 35.4, 34.5, 31.2, 29.4, 21.9, 15.0, 14.7. Clear decline. So reproducible. One fact could show mean joy count per decade declining while joy is highest overall.\n\nAlternatively \"average amount of each emotion in top 10 hits over time\" chart: small multiples. The claim \"confluence of increases in sadness, fear, disgust, and anger\" is actually false in absolute mean counts: decade means of sadness 24.0→10.1, fear 25.2→10.0, disgust 14.8→6.3, anger 19.1→8.8 all decline from 1960s to 2010s. But relative shares may increase. Let's compute shares per decade. The story says \"increases in sadness, fear, disgust, and anger\" and joy relative decline. The counts per song decline overall across all emotions (lyrics length? NRC lexicons; data maybe). Relative proportions might show increases. Let's compute.","thinkingSignature":