{"type":"message","id":"8e908421","parentId":"573e739b","timestamp":"2026-10-28T21:06:43.345Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Relative shares: anger, disgust, fear, sadness rise from 1960s to 2020s; joy declines. This matches \"confluence of increases in sadness, fear, disgust, and anger\" and \"Joy ... relative decline.\" Good. But from 1960s to 2010s: anger 9.2→11.1, disgust 7.1→8.0, fear 12.1→12.6, sadness 11.5→12.8 (by corpus share) and joy 17.0→19.1 actually increases, though 2020s 15.4. Mean per-song share joy declines 28.6→16.4. Hmm.\n\nThe emotions are counts per song, not normalized. \"Average amount of each emotion\" in the small multiples (counts) declines for all. The story's claim about increases may not be supported by absolute counts, but is supported by relative share only in 2020s vs 1960s. Let's compute 1960s share vs 2020s share: anger 9.2→13.1, disgust 7.1→8.8, fear 12.1→15.5, sadness 11.5→13.2, joy 17.0→15.4. That's a clean finding: relative prevalence of each emotion as share of all emotion words; the four negative emotions up, joy down. But the story's temporal scope \"over the decades\"/\"recent\" and methodology top 10 from 1960 to 2020. Using 2020s decade includes only 2020 (since filter <=2020), so label would be \"1960s vs 2020\". Fine.\n\nBut the fact must be code printing all items at once: e.g., group by decade and compute emotion share. Claim: \"As a share of all emotion words in top-10 songs, anger rose from 9.2% in the 1960s to 13.1% in 2020, disgust from 7.1% to 8.8%, fear from 12.1% to 15.5%, and sadness from 11.5% to 13.2%, while joy fell from 17.0% to 15.4%.\" Code: group by decade, sum emotions, normalize. Printout shows. Good.\n\nHowever, are counts the right unit? The NRC raw_emotion_scores gives counts of words associated with each emotion. To compare across time, share of emotion words is reasonable. But the story says \"average amount of each emotion\" and \"proportion of negative emotions\". For hip hop, \"higher proportion of negative emotions\". Relative share is the story's concept.\n\nFact 3: \"Joy is consistently the most prevalent emotion across the years, followed by trust and anticipation.\" In share terms by decade: 1960 joy 17.0 < trust 19.0; 1970 joy 18.2 > trust 17.9; 1980 joy 17.9 < trust 18.3; 1990 joy 18.0 > trust 16.6; 2000 joy 17.4 > trust 16.6; 2010 joy 19.1 > trust 13.9; 2020 joy 15.4 > trust 11.8. So \"consistently the most prevalent\" is false for 1960s and 1980s by corpus share (trust higher). By mean count per song, overall joy 29.31 vs trust 29.27, and by year? Let's check per-year highest emotion mean. Could be joy mostly. The process summary says joy consistently most prevalent, followed by trust and anticipation; sadness most common negative, followed by fear; anger/disgust less. Let me compute per-year top mean emotion 1960-2020.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_00_llFoGEoyiFWSMP4GrGVn4928","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/user/RL_Study/report-gepa/runs/originals/ai && python -c \"\nimport pandas as pd, numpy as np, ast\ndf=pd.read_csv('data/billboard.csv'); df['year']=pd.to_datetime(df.chart_week).dt.year\ne=df.Emotions.apply(ast.literal_eval); em=['anger','anticipation','disgust','fear','joy','sadness','surprise','trust']\nfor m in em: df[m]=e.apply(lambda x: x.get(m,0))\nd=df[(df.year>=1960)&(df.year<=2020)]\ntop=d.groupby('year')[em].mean().idxmax()\nprint(top.value_counts())\nprint('years not joy:', list(top[top!='joy'].items()))\nprint('overall sums', d[em].sum().sort_values(ascending=False).to_dict())\nprint('overall means', d[em].mean().sort_values(ascending=False).round(2).to_dict())\n# negative emotions order\nprint('neg mean', d[['sadness','fear','anger','disgust']].mean().round(2).to_dict())\n\""}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"deepseek","model":"deepseek-flash","usage":{"input":804,"output":1111,"cacheRead":75392,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens