nger and disgust are less frequent.\" — Partially false (fear > sadness overall). Could include bits that hold.\nP4. \"2018 having an almost 50-50 split between positive and negative songs.\" — 55.6/44.4, \"almost 50-50\". Reproducible.\nP5. \"2020 ... skewed more positive than the couple years prior.\" — 2020 50.8% positive vs 2018 44.4%, 2019 62.2%. Not more positive than both (less than 2019). False.\nP6. \"Eminem's rap has a lot more negativity and anger compared to Drake's\" — no genre but artist sentiment: Eminem 73% neg vs Drake 48%. Could reproduce; anger mean? Let's check. But \"rap\" genre absent. Eminem vs Drake sentiment is computable.\nP7. \"Billie Eilish's pop is more negative than the norm for that genre\" — genre absent.\nP8. Most joyful hit \"Happy\" 2014 — can compute joy count/max. Let's check.\nP9. Other highly positive hits: Love On Top, Can't Stop the Feeling, Shake It Off — check.\nP10. \"most emotionally negative #1 hit is Lil Nas X's 'Panini' in 2019\" — check.\nP11. Other negative songs: Emotionally Scarred, Mona Lisa, Jail — check.\nP12. \"60-70% positive\" etc.\n\nThese are Claude's findings, not the authors'. The benchmark may treat Claude's analysis summary as part of the article's story? Hmm. The page title is \"Can an AI make a data-driven, visual story?\"; the AI story is the data story; Claude's analysis is a step in the process. The phrase \"the story's findings\" more likely refers to the AI's story, because that's the only part that draws findings from billboard.csv in narrative form. Claude's analysis summary also draws findings from the data, but it's presented as an intermediate step and the authors say \"Here is a sample of the data, analyze it\" and quote the output. The authors do not assert them. The task says \"the findings its authors drew from their data\" — the authors = Pudding writers. They drew meta-findings about Claude, not music. So the target must be the AI story, whose findings are presented as the published da